indium (2.5.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A correct password is accepted on an encrypted 7z that INDIUM made. Every encrypted 7z
    it writes hides the file names as well as the contents, and the check that decides
    whether a password is right asked a reader that cannot see past those names. It
    answered "the headers are encrypted", the check read that as "wrong password", and it
    did so for every archive of the kind this program produces. The check now asks the
    reader that can answer, before the one that cannot.
  * Removing a member from an archive rooted at `./` no longer destroys the rest of it.
    That is the shape `tar -cf x.tar -C dir .` makes, which is the commonest way a tar gets
    written. The root has no name once normalised, so the list INDIUM planned against had
    one fewer element than the list it walked, and it paired them by position: every
    payload was written under its neighbour's name and the last member fell off the end and
    was not written at all. Apply reported success, and the check that should have caught
    it compares sizes only for regular files -- so the one member destroyed outright was
    the one member the check skipped. Both halves are gated now, and the rebuilt archive
    comes out unrooted deliberately rather than by accident.
  * Extraction cannot write outside the folder that was chosen. The guard against it was a
    check on the member's stored name, plus two protections the archive reader applies --
    and the reader that handles 7z archives with encrypted names is a different one, which
    applies neither. The stored name did not have to escape anything: extracting an
    ordinary tar that carries a symlink plants that symlink on disk, which is normal and
    succeeds, and extracting into the same folder afterwards followed it. Extraction now
    walks down to each file one directory at a time, refusing a link where a directory
    should be, and writes by removing the name first and creating it exclusively -- which
    also refuses a hard link, where refusing to follow a symlink would not have.
  * INDIUM no longer refuses to extract an archive it wrote itself. A member stored as
    `./alpha.txt` was read as an attempt to climb out of the destination, because the check
    rejected every part of a path that was not a plain name, and `.` is the way a path says
    "here". A name climbing out with `..`, and an absolute name, are still refused.
  * Extracting over a file whose permissions you had tightened keeps them tightened. It had
    been resetting them to the default, which the previous way of writing the file happened
    to preserve and the new one did not.
  * A rebuild keeps the archive's permissions, and never widens them while it works. An
    archive readable only by its owner was rebuilt into a working copy that anyone on the
    machine could read, sitting beside the original under a name that could be guessed, for
    as long as the rebuild took -- measured at two thirds of a second on a 64 MiB archive,
    and for an encrypted one that working copy is the ciphertext. The permissions are now
    set before the first byte is written rather than after the last. The settings file is
    saved the same way, and for the same reason.
  * Removing a member from an archive whose file names are encrypted says what is wrong.
    INDIUM cannot rebuild one of those yet, and the message it gave asked for a password
    that had already been given and was already correct.
  * The temporary names this program makes in the shared temporary directory are its own.
    Four were predictable enough that another account on the same machine could occupy the
    name first. Rather than four separate corrections, a check now reads the whole tree and
    refuses a fifth.
  * A lock file this account cannot write is still a lock file. It had been read as an
    absent one, so a second window could open on an archive the first was already
    rebuilding.
  * The notice that appears when the settings file cannot be read no longer promises a copy
    that is not there. It said "a copy is at" whenever the name was occupied, including
    when what occupied it was a link pointing at nothing.
  * No feature and no redesign. The window is v2.3's, the formats are the same eight, and
    the estimator returns the same figures. This is the round that reads the code rather
    than adding to it: 416 tests pass and ten more wait on a published package, every fix
    that could have blocked the release was read by a party that did not write it, and the
    faults judged too large to repair safely this close to a freeze are written down in the
    round's document rather than left for the next reader to find.

 -- sudo-megas <sudo-megas@users.noreply.github.com>  Tue, 18 Aug 2026 21:00:00 +0300

indium (2.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The window is drawn in Cascadia Mono rather than Fira Mono. Both are monospaced and
    both carry the icon set INDIUM draws from; the swap is the maker's call about the face
    he wants to read. The `Mono` cut is the one that ships and a test now says why: the
    proportional cut draws its icons two cells wide, which would leave every column in the
    entry table half a character out without anything looking broken enough to report.
  * Zones have corners. Every pane -- the sidebar, the entry table, the Inspector, the
    status bar -- was a square-cornered rectangle and is now rounded by 6px. The window
    still uses three radii and no fourth; the zone's was the one that had been zero.
  * A zone casts a warm glow into the gutter it floats in, which is the depth cue that
    tells a pane sitting above the background apart from one painted onto it. It is
    bounded so it cannot become decoration: one zone's cast never reaches its neighbour,
    and its strength is measured against the gutter rather than judged by eye.
  * Text is set at six named levels rather than eight numbers spread across nine files.
    Two of them moved: a pane's heading and a chooser row go from 14 to 15, which puts two
    points between a heading and the value beneath it instead of one point and a weight,
    and Measure's heading comes down from 16 to join them.
  * Popups can be dragged. All eight -- About, Keys, Settings, Extract, Create, Open With,
    Pending tasks and Open archive -- had been pinned to the middle of the window by a call
    that also refused the mouse, so the centring and the immovability were one thing and
    were never separated. They still open centred, they now go where they are put, and a
    popup that has been moved reopens where it was left until the program closes. Password
    and Measure are modal and stay fixed.
  * The Space row of the Keys popup drew an empty box. The arrow it asked for is in
    neither weight of either face, old or new, so it has been a box in every release that
    had a Keys popup to open. It is an arrow that exists now, and a test walks every
    character the program can draw against both faces so a second one cannot appear.
  * Apply and Create fill with the accent instead of tinting a word with it. Orange as ink
    measured 3.65:1 on the face it was painted on -- under the 4.5 every other pairing in
    the window holds to -- and as a fill under dark text it reads 5.40, rising as the
    button is hovered and pressed rather than dimming.
  * The remove mark is one character with one name. Four of them had been typed into the
    layout rather than drawn from the icon set, so they came from the text part of the
    face and sat at a different weight to the marks beside them.
  * Nothing changed about what the program does: the same eight formats, the same
    estimator and its figures, the same rebuild, the same ten popups. This is the round
    that redraws the window, and the walk against it is what decides whether it ships.

 -- sudo-megas <sudo-megas@users.noreply.github.com>  Fri, 14 Aug 2026 23:30:00 +0300

indium (2.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Archives made with `tar -cf x.tar -C dir .` extract again. Their first member is the
    archive root `./`, whose name normalises to nothing, and a guard written for a
    different defect read that empty name as one that could not be decoded -- so INDIUM
    refused the whole archive and blamed the system it was being read on. It is the
    commonest tar shape there is. A member that is exactly the archive root is now told
    apart from a name that genuinely did not survive the read, and the second is still
    refused whole-archive, exactly as before.
  * A rebuild or a creation into a folder that is not there says so, and says which
    folder. It used to name a temporary file nobody had chosen -- something ending
    `.indium-new` -- and it only did that for `.7z`, because that was the format anybody
    had watched fail. The check sits in front of both writers now, so all seven writable
    containers answer the same way.
  * A long Apply says where it is, and stops when it is told to. Progress and the cancel
    flag were only read between members, which is nothing to go on when the archive is
    one large member: the program was working the whole time and had no way to say so.
    Both land inside the member now, throttled to about a hundred reports over a member
    rather than a million, and a cancelled run clears up what it had written.
  * Preselect is a button, and it is third in its row. It had been the row's label,
    sitting first, at the same size as two words that were pressable -- which makes a
    third pressable word whatever it was meant to be. It does what it was read as doing:
    it asks for a directory once, and sends every archive there until another mode is
    picked.
  * Two things stop being cut off in a narrow window. The Inspector yields to the entry
    table rather than holding its full width, so the table keeps all four columns; and
    the draft's button row wraps, which stops the refusal sentence, the empty-state text
    and the staged rows beneath it being drawn into space the panel then throws away.
  * Text is evenly spaced again, and the cursor row's filename is legible. A line
    switching off sub-pixel binning had been added on the belief that a monospace face
    gives a whole-pixel advance; Fira Mono's is 0.600 em, which at 13 px is 7.8 px, so
    the gaps ran 8, 8, 7, 8, 8, 8. And the filename on the cursor row was painted in the
    accent, on a ground already washed with the accent, at 2.91:1 -- it is the ordinary
    text ink now, at 9.14:1.
  * Nothing else changed in the program: the same eight formats, the same estimator and
    its figures, the same rebuild, the same ten popups. This is the round that walked the
    shipped 2.1 package end to end -- 158 steps, every denial answered -- so what changed
    here is what that walk found.

