indium (2.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Files first, Create last. Making an archive used to begin with the popup that asks
    how to compress it -- before there were any files to compress, and before the
    directory to add them from was even open, so the popup had to be answered blind and
    then answered again. There is a Draft now: a second section of the entry table,
    reached with 2 or from the sidebar, holding what the next archive will be made of.
    Add files to it, then press N once. The Measure action is live on that popup's first
    frame, because by then there are real bytes to weigh.
  * Bring from archive copies the entries you have selected in the open archive straight
    into the draft. They become real files at that moment, so the archive they came from
    can be closed, renamed or rebuilt without touching them.
  * An archive is something you can close. A Close control sits on the breadcrumb row,
    beside Add files.... Opening another archive from inside INDIUM now takes the same
    window, having closed the one it held -- a file manager or a command line naming a
    second archive still gets a second window, because two programs asking for two
    windows means two. Both doors say what closing cost: "Closed photos.zip - 4 staged
    changes discarded." A staged creation is not one of those changes and survives, since
    it names an archive the closed file was never the subject of.
  * The sidebar is three groups rather than two, and the number keys follow the order
    they are drawn in: File 1, Draft 2, Recent files 3, Bookmarks 4. Create moves up
    beside the file and the draft it builds from; Open file moves down beside the lists.
    The File view can be entered with nothing open, where it says so and offers the way
    in.
  * Nothing else changed in the program: the same eight formats, the same estimator and
    its figures, the same rebuild, the same ten popups.

 -- sudo-megas <sudo-megas@users.noreply.github.com>  Thu, 13 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0300

indium (2.0.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The New Archive popup measures instead of asserting. A Measure action runs all eight
    compression methods over the files you have actually staged — or over the archive you
    have open — on this machine, and opens a popup of its own holding each one's level,
    elapsed time, resulting size and ratio in a table you can read down a column. The
    sentences stay where they were: a number says how much, a sentence says what for.
    Clicking a row chooses that method.
  * The figures are exact where they can be. Inputs of 2 MiB or less are compressed
    whole, through the same writers a rebuild uses, so the size shown is the size a
    rebuild would produce. Larger inputs are sampled across their whole length rather
    than from the front, and every figure taken from a sample is marked with a leading
    "~" — sampling predicts speed well and gzip and zstd's ratios closely, but it cannot
    predict xz's, whose dictionary depends on matches a sample necessarily breaks up.
  * The candidates run one at a time and can be stopped. Closing the popup ends a
    measurement, and starting a rebuild or an extraction takes priority over one.
    Nothing is ever encrypted to measure it, and no password is ever asked for.
  * New Archive can be re-opened over a staged creation without discarding the files
    already added to it, and shows the recipe that is actually staged.
  * Text renders without sub-pixel binning. The window is monospace throughout, so the
    even kerning it buys was already there, and the blur it costs was not worth paying.

